Helios patch 0003 asan-unpoison-freed-page (Luau 0.739; WP-0.10, RT-13's ASan-clean script_tests)

Under AddressSanitizer, Luau poisons the blocks of its allocator pages that are not in use (newpage,
freeblock, freegcoblock) and unpoisons a block when it hands it out. freepage then returns an empty page
to lua_Alloc with those blocks still poisoned. With ASan's own malloc that is harmless: free() does not
touch the block and a later malloc() marks it addressable again. Any other host allocator owns the memory
again and may write it, or give it to its next allocation still marked poisoned. mimalloc (behind
helios::alignedFree) fills freed blocks with 0xDF in debug builds, so every ScriptVm::create in the
linux-debug-asan build failed with use-after-poison in mi_free the first time a page emptied (the old
string table freed by luaS_resize during luaT_init). This patch unpoisons the whole page before freepage
returns it (the page header was never poisoned). Outside ASan builds ASAN_UNPOISON_MEMORY_REGION expands
to (void)0, so nothing changes there.

Rebase notes: every path that returns a lua_Page to frealloc or cagealloc goes through freepage. An
upstream change that frees pages elsewhere needs the same unpoison.

Upstream status: not submitted. Upstream master's freepage is unchanged, and no upstream issue was found.
Rebase on every Luau bump (K10), and delete the patch once upstream unpoisons freed pages. Licence: MIT
(Helios, like Luau).

diff --git a/VM/src/lmem.cpp b/VM/src/lmem.cpp
index cc3f8bd..ee95afd 100644
--- a/VM/src/lmem.cpp
+++ b/VM/src/lmem.cpp
@@ -350,6 +350,11 @@ static void freepage(lua_State* L, lua_Page** pageset, lua_Page* page)
             *pageset = page->listnext;
     }
 
+    // Helios patch asan-unpoison-freed-page: the page goes back to the allocator addressable. Its free blocks are still
+    // poisoned, and an allocator that is not ASan's own may write freed memory (debug fills, its own metadata) or hand
+    // it to its next allocation, which ASan then reports as use-after-poison.
+    ASAN_UNPOISON_MEMORY_REGION(page, size_t(page->pageSize));
+
     // so long
     pagealloc<path, type>(g, page, page->pageSize, 0);
 }
